{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-344629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $b$ that minimizes the distance between the points $(5b+3,\\,b-1)$ and $(4,\\,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =((5b+3)-4)^{2}+((b-1)-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(5b-1)^{2}+(b-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(25{b}^{2}-10b+1)+({b}^{2}-6b+9) \\\\  & =26{b}^{2}-16b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=52b-16.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 52b-16 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{4}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(b)=52>0.$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{4}{13}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-344629" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344629" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{13}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the distance between the points $(a+1,a)$ and $(1,-2)$ is minimized, then $a=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a+1-1)^{2}+(a-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(a)^{2}+(a+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+{a}^{2}+4a+4 \\\\  & =2{a}^{2}+4a+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =4a+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 4a+4 & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(-1)=4>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-117431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the distance between the points $(3a-4,a+2)$ and $(-2,6)$ is minimized, then $a=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={(3a-4-(-2))}^{2}+{(a+2-6)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3a-2)^{2}+(a-4)^{2} \\\\  & =9{a}^{2}-12a+4+{a}^{2}-8a+16 \\\\  & =10{a}^{2}-20a+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =20a-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 20a-20 & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(1)=20>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-210773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "10"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the distance between the points $(b-2,2b)$ and $(1,-1)$ is minimized, then $b=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =(b-2-1)^{2}+{(2b-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={b}^{2}-6b+9+4{b}^{2}+4b+1 \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-2b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =10b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5})=10>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-210769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $a$ that minimizes the distance between the points $(a,\\,-2a)$ and $(4,\\,-3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a-4)^{2}+((-2a)-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(a-4)^{2}+(-2a+3)^{2} \\\\  & =({a}^{2}-8a+16)+(4{a}^{2}-12a+9) \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-20a+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=10a-20.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-20 & =0 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(a)=10>0.$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-316014" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316014" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((-4x)-1)}^{2}+{((2x-3)-5)}^{2} \\\\  & =(-4x-1)^{2}+(2x-8)^{2} \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}+8x+1+4{x}^{2}-32x+64 \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-24x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=40x-24.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40x-24 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{3}{5})=40>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-24(\\frac{3}{5})+65} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{289}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5)$ is $\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-344637" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344637" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((4x)-5)}^{2}+{((-x)-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(4x-5)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(16{x}^{2}-40x+25)+({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =17{x}^{2}-42x+26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=34x-42.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 34x-42 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{21}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{21}{17})=34>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{21}{17}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{21}{17}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{(\\frac{21}{17})}^{2}-42(\\frac{21}{17})+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{441}{17}-\\frac{882}{17}+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1)$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-344635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0),$ where $b$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & ={(b-3)}^{2}+{(2b-0)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b)^{2} \\\\  & =({b}^{2}-6b+9)+(4{b}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-6b+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=10b-6.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-6 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{3}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(b)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $b=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-6(\\frac{3}{5})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}-\\frac{18}{5}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0)$ is $\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-316023" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-316023" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4),$ where $a$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={((2a-1)-3)}^{2}+{(a-(-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2a-4)^{2}+(a+4)^{2} \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-16a+16+{a}^{2}+8a+16 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-8a+32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=10a-8.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-8 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{4}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{4}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{4}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}-8(\\frac{4}{5})+32} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{144}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4)$ is $\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-344638" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344638" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(a,2a-1)$ and $(4,-2),$ where $a$ is a real number?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={(a-4)}^{2}+{(2a-1-(-2))}^{2} \\\\  & =(a-4)^{2}+(2a+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}-8a+16+4{a}^{2}+4a+1 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-4a+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =10a-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{2}{5})=10>0.$ So, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{2}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{2}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}-4(\\frac{2}{5})+17} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-210779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-210779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use calculus to find the coordinates of the closest point to $P(4,5)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-7,2)$ and $B(3,2).$\n\nThe coordinates of the required point are\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/e283660466c42f7723d9e044bb2eefe148c468f7dae5a1b5882e2589e7e5f60a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(4,5)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-10y+41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-7,2)$ and $B(3,2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+49+(y-2)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}}+14x+49 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-6x+9+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 14x+6x & =9-49 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(-2)-10y+41 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}+16-10y+41 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-10y+61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y-10,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-10 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(5)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=5$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,-2,5\\,).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-344721" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344721" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344721" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344721" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(1,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/052b405c104f0d6bc353782c6e07bfb1e0245726d86346a545ea38aa31941711.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(1,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-2))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-87502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,2)"] ["math" "(0,0)"] ["math" "(0,1)"] ["math" "(0,2)"] ["math" "(1,-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use calculus to find the coordinates of the closest point to $P(5,-3)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$\n\nThe coordinates of the required point are\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/a86fbc456930e18159b1745f5a71fe82bf7075f3aa6d9fd4307a71a583a37a7a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(5,-3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10x+6y+34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+(y-1)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-12x+36+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-12x+36+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ 4x+12x & =36-4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-10(2)+6y+34 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}-20+6y+34 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+6y+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y+6,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(-3)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=-3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,2,-3\\,).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-344720" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344720" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344720" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-344720" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(6,7)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-117463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(3,\\frac{7}{2})"] ["math" "(6,3)"] ["math" "(6,4)"] ["math" "(6,\\frac{7}{2})"] ["math" "(\\frac{7}{2},6)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-117463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(6,\\frac{7}{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(2,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/8be39e6dde084154ecfd43718cb76352c69d26bd76d520177a6c41ee69a7f18e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(2,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-4y+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-8x+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4y+20 \\\\ 8y & =8x-16 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(x-2)^{2}-4x-4\\cdot (x-2)+8 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+4-4x-4x+8+8 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-12x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(3)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(3,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bac60e18d1615dc6047085e4ffe9cd3a0f67b1b91c45db8988353561911d48fa"} {"question" "q-87515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(3,1)"] ["math" "(3,2)"] ["math" "(3,3)"] ["math" "(4,1)"] ["math" "(4,2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "q-87515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/state.json" "file_sha256" "4fce9e1c29bf395f1e8c59a38e244e66bfb86bd3d73a178b7d3ceda69656c126"} {"question" "e-12605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the distance between the points $(b+3,b)$ and $(-1, 2)$ is minimized, find the value of $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/example-12605.json" "file_sha256" "079dd6b9e21ac78ac002d8890301f878f7c0b638f800c7606031196145d3e36e"} {"question" "e-12605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =(b+3-(-1))^{2}+{(b-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b+4)^{2}+(b-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+8b+16+{b}^{2}-4b+4 \\\\  & =2{b}^{2}+4b+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative $S'(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =4b+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative $S'(b) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 4b+4 & =0 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''\\left(-1\\right) =4> 0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b = -1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/example-12605.json" "file_sha256" "079dd6b9e21ac78ac002d8890301f878f7c0b638f800c7606031196145d3e36e"} {"question" "e-12594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(a+1, a+2)$ and $(-1, 3),$ where $a$ is a real number?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/example-12594.json" "file_sha256" "8ea60fcedc37133a9230f7e5c5e8282c2b69fbc0f3ae1e6c6bcfe33b1d5395b0"} {"question" "e-12594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a+1-(-1))^{2}+(a+2-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(a+2)^{2}+(a-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+4a+4+{a}^{2}-2a+1 \\\\  & =2{a}^{2}+2a+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative $S'(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =4a+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative $S'(a) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 4a+2 & =0 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''\\left(- \\dfrac 12\\right) =4 > 0.$ So, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a = -\\dfrac12.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=-\\dfrac 12$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S\\left(-\\dfrac 12\\right)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{2})+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/example-12594.json" "file_sha256" "8ea60fcedc37133a9230f7e5c5e8282c2b69fbc0f3ae1e6c6bcfe33b1d5395b0"} {"question" "e-12595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(3,-2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/example-12595.json" "file_sha256" "b639b9b552d8eeadb4adf9ddb2a51a2a734b3feb0d4bb55fd5c2b30242623a4d"} {"question" "e-12595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/a22e7e19b360c91bcdae8eba8a3230943bd69270eac9dd158652d1648e24de51.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(3,-2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+4y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-4x+4+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-2y+2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ 6x-2 & =2y \\\\ y & =3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(3x-1)^{2}-6x+4(3x-1)+13 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}-6x+1-6x+12x-4+13 \\\\  & =10{x}^{2}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =20x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(0) =20>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny = 3(0)-1 = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(0,-1\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/example-12595.json" "file_sha256" "b639b9b552d8eeadb4adf9ddb2a51a2a734b3feb0d4bb55fd5c2b30242623a4d"}] "usage" {"e-12605" [] "e-12595" [] "e-12594" []} "basis" 2274 "retractions" [[17592186100504 :question/problem "If the distance between the points $(b+3,b)$ and $(-1, 2)$ is minimized, find the value of $b$."] [17592186100504 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(b) &= (b + 3 - (-1))^{2} + (b - 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= (b + 4)^{2} + (b - 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= b^{2} + 8b + 16 + b^{2} - 4b + 4 \\\\\n&= 2b^{2} + 4b + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative $S'(b)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(b) &= 4b + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative $S'(b) = 0$. Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(b) &= 0 \\\\\n4b + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nb &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that\n\n$$\nS''\\left(-1\\right) =4> 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b = -1$."] [17592186100501 :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(a+1, a+2)$ and $(-1, 3)$, where $a$ is a real number?"] [17592186100501 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(a) &= (a + 1 - (-1))^{2} + (a + 2 - 3)^{2} \\\\\n&= (a + 2)^{2} + (a - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= a^{2} + 4a + 4 + a^{2} - 2a + 1 \\\\\n&= 2a^{2} + 2a + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative $S'(a)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(a) &= 4a + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative $S'(a) = 0$. Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(a) &= 0 \\\\\n4a + 2 &= 0 \\\\\na &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that\n\n$$\nS''\\left(-\\dfrac 12\\right) =4 > 0\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S\\left(-\\dfrac 12\\right)}\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{S(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} + 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) + 5} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100502 :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(3,-2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0)$."] [17592186100502 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1216/Source/Images/9cb086844bb8ee036071225f1feb853f.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}$.\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B$.\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P$.\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(3,-2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(x, y) &= (x - 3)^{2} + (y - (-2))^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 6x + 9 + y^{2} + 4y + 4 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + y^{2} - 6x + 4y + 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0)$. So, by writing down\n\n$$\n(QA)^2 = (QB)^2\n$$\n\nwe have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - (-1))^{2} + (y - 1)^{2}_(⏟)_((QA)^{2}) &= (x - 2)^{2} + (y - 0)^{2}_(⏟)_((QB)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 1 + y^{2} - 2y + 1 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 + y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 1 + y^{2} - 2y + 1 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 + y^{2} \\\\\n2x - 2y + 2 &= -4x + 4 \\\\\n6x - 2 &= 2y \\\\\ny &= 3x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(x) &= x^{2} + (3x - 1)^{2} - 6x + 4(3x - 1) + 13 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 9x^{2} - 6x + 1 - 6x + 12x - 4 + 13 \\\\\n&= 10x^{2} + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) &= 20x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n20x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(0) =20>0\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance.\n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny = 3(0)-1 = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is\n\n$$\n\\left(0,-1\\right)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
